WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 99:59:59.999
Phase 11, Advanced AI engineering. Once the system works, the questions change: how fast, how cheap, how many users, and what happens when a dependency fails? This phase is about performance and failure under real traffic. This phase has 3 lessons. Lesson 1: Optimize inference. Optimization is a measurement loop: define a target, profile the actual bottleneck, change one thing, and verify both performance and quality. The question for this lesson: "Where are time. Fast is a measurement, not a feeling. A service that feels snappy on your laptop can melt under twenty concurrent requests, and a model that takes 40 milliseconds on average can still ruin someone's day at the 99th percentile. Profile before you optimize. The bottleneck is often not where the intuition points: it might be JSON parsing, a pandas copy, a cold model load, or a database call pretending to be a model problem. You will learn: Latency and throughput are different measures., p95 and p99 show tail pain that averages hide., Batching, caching, quantization, and compilation help only in the right workload.. Practice: "Measure p50/p95 latency and throughput under realistic concurrency. Profile one bottleneck. Watch out for: Optimizing without a profile., Using a one-request local benchmark as a capacity plan., Shipping a faster service with worse predictions.. You are ready when: Set latency and quality targets., Profile first., Load test realistically.. Recommended video: Profiling and optimizing PyTorch applications. Lesson 2: Scale inference and data paths. Scaling is a queueing problem, not just a bigger server. Stateless services, batching, caching, backpressure, and capacity estimates decide the knee. The question for this lesson: What breaks first when traffic grows?. At low traffic, average latency looks fine. Under load, queues form, tail latency explodes, and retries make the queue longer. The useful question is not “how many requests per second?” but “what is the latency at the traffic we actually expect?” Scale the part that is actually constrained. A stateless API can add replicas; a stateful model server may need batching or a different runtime. Caches help repeated work, but they also create freshness and invalidation problems. You will learn: Queueing turns small utilization increases into large tail latency., Batching raises throughput but can hurt per-request latency., Backpressure protects the system when demand exceeds capacity.. Practice: "Load test the API. Watch out for: Scaling a stateful service by copying it., Letting a queue grow without a limit or alert., Testing with one request and calling it capacity.. You are ready when: Measure p95 under load., Find the knee., Add backpressure.. Lesson 3: Reliability under failure. Timeouts, retries with jitter, idempotency, circuit breakers, and graceful degradation keep one failure from becoming an outage. The question for this lesson: "What happens when a dependency is slow. Distributed systems fail partially. A database is slow, a model server returns errors, a third-party API times out. Without a timeout, a request waits forever. Without jitter, every client retries at the same instant and creates a thundering herd. Reliability is designed, then tested. Define an SLO, add a fallback for non-critical paths, and deliberately break a dependency in staging. A failure you have rehearsed is an incident; one you have not is a surprise. You will learn: Timeouts are required at every network boundary., Retries need limits, backoff, jitter, and idempotency., Graceful degradation beats a perfect response that never arrives.. Practice: "Kill the model dependency in staging. Verify the timeout. Watch out for: Retrying non-idempotent writes., No timeout on an HTTP client., Alerting on every transient error until nobody reads alerts.. You are ready when: Set timeouts., Test fallback., Review alert ownership.. A latency budget, a scaling plan, and a failure mode you have actually tested. Take your time with each lesson and practice the examples.
